1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,050
After an edit to the skill, seven of ten
trials passing becomes nine of ten.

2
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:09,711
That looks like an improvement, but the
same model with the same request gives

3
00:00:09,711 --> 00:00:11,525
different results from run to run.

4
00:00:12,025 --> 00:00:16,384
So some of that gain may be chance, and
we've already edited this skill four times

5
00:00:16,384 --> 00:00:17,925
on counts smaller than these.

6
00:00:18,425 --> 00:00:22,730
Here, you'll make one more edit, measure
the skill before and after it, and say in

7
00:00:22,730 --> 00:00:24,725
one sentence what the result supports.

8
00:00:25,933 --> 00:00:29,408
Let's start with what a count from a few
trials can tell us.

9
00:00:29,908 --> 00:00:34,008
When seven of ten trials pass, seventy
percent is an estimate.

10
00:00:34,508 --> 00:00:37,908
Ten more trials might give five passes, or
nine.

11
00:00:38,408 --> 00:00:42,908
The true pass rate is the one we'd see
over a very large number of trials.

12
00:00:43,408 --> 00:00:48,033
All we know is a range for it: probably
between forty and eighty-nine percent.

13
00:00:48,533 --> 00:00:52,727
Probably means that a range worked out
this way holds the true rate about

14
00:00:52,727 --> 00:00:54,508
ninety-five times in a hundred.

15
00:00:55,733 --> 00:00:59,508
Now back to the edit that took seven of
ten to nine of ten.

16
00:01:00,008 --> 00:01:03,963
Here are the two ranges, forty to
eighty-nine, and sixty to ninety-eight,

17
00:01:03,963 --> 00:01:05,858
and they overlap almost completely.

18
00:01:06,358 --> 00:01:10,677
If the skill's true pass rate were eighty
percent both before and after, it could

19
00:01:10,677 --> 00:01:12,383
easily produce those two counts.

20
00:01:12,883 --> 00:01:15,508
So this result doesn't show a change.

21
00:01:16,008 --> 00:01:20,171
With the same two rates over a hundred
trials each, the ranges don't touch, and

22
00:01:20,171 --> 00:01:21,383
that would be evidence.

23
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:24,825
Three habits follow from that.

24
00:01:25,325 --> 00:01:29,729
First, we compare like with like: the same
stimuli, the same graders and the same

25
00:01:29,729 --> 00:01:32,175
model, with both versions run in one
sitting.

26
00:01:32,675 --> 00:01:36,782
Second, we report counts, and not only
percentages, because one hundred percent

27
00:01:36,782 --> 00:01:39,850
could be three of three, or three hundred
of three hundred.

28
00:01:40,350 --> 00:01:44,575
Third, when the ranges overlap, we say
that these trials cannot tell.

29
00:01:45,075 --> 00:01:49,406
That doesn't mean the edit did nothing; it
means we don't know yet, and more trials

30
00:01:49,406 --> 00:01:50,450
are how to find out.

31
00:01:51,667 --> 00:01:56,610
More trials narrow a range, but slowly: it
takes about four times the trials to halve

32
00:01:56,610 --> 00:01:57,192
its width.

33
00:01:57,692 --> 00:02:01,579
That's the real price of certainty, and
the reason to spend trials where a

34
00:02:01,579 --> 00:02:02,892
decision depends on them.

35
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:08,525
The arithmetic is tedious, so the course
has a small calculator for it.

36
00:02:09,025 --> 00:02:13,375
It takes a count before and a count after,
and shows the range around each.

37
00:02:13,875 --> 00:02:17,675
Then it says whether the two ranges
overlap, which here they do.

38
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:22,750
That's how to read a count; here's the
experiment that will give us some.

39
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:27,519
It's an experiment file, which names
variants of one eval, and this one comes

40
00:02:27,519 --> 00:02:28,850
with the starter folder.

41
00:02:29,350 --> 00:02:34,325
The eval is the one graded by our judge:
three requests worded in coding terms.

42
00:02:34,825 --> 00:02:39,407
Runs, set to three under overrides, gives
every variant three trials of each

43
00:02:39,407 --> 00:02:39,950
stimulus.

44
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:44,075
Both of our models, haiku and terra, get
two variants each.

45
00:02:44,575 --> 00:02:49,028
One uses a copy of the skill as it is
before our edit, in a folder named

46
00:02:49,028 --> 00:02:50,450
changelog entry before.

47
00:02:50,950 --> 00:02:54,825
Its partner uses the skill itself, which
we're about to edit.

48
00:02:56,033 --> 00:03:00,447
When the experiment runs, each stimulus
that wasn't a clean pass gets a block,

49
00:03:00,447 --> 00:03:02,258
headed by its variant and model.

50
00:03:02,758 --> 00:03:06,558
With a judge, the block lists the judge's
verdict for each trial.

51
00:03:07,058 --> 00:03:11,143
Here are some from a run of this
experiment, cut down to their first two

52
00:03:11,143 --> 00:03:11,483
lines.

53
00:03:11,983 --> 00:03:16,233
After the stimulus name comes the count of
trials that passed and failed.

54
00:03:16,733 --> 00:03:21,283
A stimulus with no block passed all of its
trials, so the blocks are enough to count

55
00:03:21,283 --> 00:03:22,583
trials for each variant.

56
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:28,750
The saved report, in the experiment's run
folder, gives the same counts another way.

57
00:03:29,250 --> 00:03:33,519
When a stimulus runs more than once, its
row gains three columns, and in their

58
00:03:33,519 --> 00:03:35,325
names, k is the number of trials.

59
00:03:35,825 --> 00:03:39,944
This row is from the same run: one
stimulus, three trials, two of which

60
00:03:39,944 --> 00:03:40,350
passed.

61
00:03:40,850 --> 00:03:45,000
Pass rate is the count: trials that
passed, out of trials that finished.

62
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:49,802
The first, pass at k, asks whether at
least one of the k trials passed, so it

63
00:03:49,802 --> 00:03:51,925
answers: can the agent do this at all?

64
00:03:52,425 --> 00:03:55,475
Here one did, so it shows one hundred
percent.

65
00:03:55,975 --> 00:04:00,640
The second, pass hat k, asks a harder
question: how likely is it that k trials

66
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:02,075
in a row would all pass?

67
00:04:02,575 --> 00:04:07,003
Passing two times in three, three times
running, comes to just under thirty

68
00:04:07,003 --> 00:04:07,475
percent.

69
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:12,600
So which column describes a skill that has
to work every time?

70
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:18,119
It's pass hat k, because a single passing
trial is enough to make pass at k show one

71
00:04:18,119 --> 00:04:19,075
hundred percent.

72
00:04:19,575 --> 00:04:23,847
If any trial ends in error, the stimulus
shows dashes in these columns, and its

73
00:04:23,847 --> 00:04:26,875
finished trials are counted in the
terminal output only.

74
00:04:27,375 --> 00:04:31,600
None of these columns gives a range, which
is what the calculator is for.

75
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:36,800
That's what the experiment prints; now
let's use it on a real edit.

76
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:41,075
Our judge found entries that still say how
a fix was coded.

77
00:04:41,575 --> 00:04:46,275
Until now, the skill's second rule only
said to write for people who use the tool.

78
00:04:46,775 --> 00:04:51,240
Our edit adds two lines to it: say what
they'll notice, and don't say how it was

79
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:51,575
coded.

80
00:04:52,075 --> 00:04:56,592
It also names what to leave out: any
library, algorithm, data structure or

81
00:04:56,592 --> 00:04:57,325
source file.

82
00:04:57,825 --> 00:05:02,575
To compare fairly, we keep a copy of the
skill as it was, beside the edited one.

83
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:08,485
Each variant runs three stimuli three
times, so every count will be out of nine

84
00:05:08,485 --> 00:05:08,900
trials.

85
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:13,838
What would you expect: will the count go
up on both models, and will nine trials be

86
00:05:13,838 --> 00:05:14,800
enough to show it?

87
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:20,175
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

88
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:25,725
First, we copy the skill folder, so the
version before the edit is kept.

89
00:05:30,207 --> 00:05:35,115
With the copy made, the new rule goes into
the skill itself, which is an edit to one

90
00:05:35,115 --> 00:05:35,407
file.

91
00:05:35,907 --> 00:05:39,482
Before spending anything, a dry run checks
the plan.

92
00:05:39,982 --> 00:05:44,232
It starts no agent, and it should list
four plans, one for each variant.

93
00:05:44,993 --> 00:05:49,115
It does: the list is longer than the
screen, and these are the last of the

94
00:05:49,115 --> 00:05:49,393
four.

95
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:04,199
Now the experiment itself: both versions
of the skill, on both models, two trials

96
00:06:04,199 --> 00:06:06,925
at a time, saved in a results folder of
its own.

97
00:06:07,425 --> 00:06:11,600
It spends thirty-six agent runs, and
thirty-six calls to the judge.

98
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:16,382
We're after the table at the end: its
stimuli row counts, for each variant, the

99
00:06:16,382 --> 00:06:18,550
stimuli that passed in all three trials.

100
00:06:20,833 --> 00:06:25,155
Several minutes later, that row reads one
of three, then three of three, for haiku,

101
00:06:25,155 --> 00:06:27,758
and zero of three, then three of three,
for terra.

102
00:06:33,258 --> 00:06:38,094
Terra's zero isn't three failures: each of
its stimuli lost a trial to an error, and

103
00:06:38,094 --> 00:06:40,108
an unfinished trial has no verdict.

104
00:06:42,608 --> 00:06:46,183
Those are counts of stimuli, and a range
needs trials.

105
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:53,907
Above the table, the output has a block
for each stimulus that wasn't a clean

106
00:06:53,907 --> 00:06:54,200
pass.

107
00:06:54,700 --> 00:06:58,719
For each variant, we add up the trials
that passed over its three stimuli,

108
00:06:58,719 --> 00:07:01,000
leaving out any trial that ended in error.

109
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:05,725
On haiku, five of nine passed before the
edit, and nine of nine after.

110
00:07:06,225 --> 00:07:10,979
Terra passed five of six before, with
three trials lost to errors, and nine of

111
00:07:10,979 --> 00:07:11,650
nine after.

112
00:07:12,867 --> 00:07:16,317
Now the range around each count, from the
calculator.

113
00:07:16,817 --> 00:07:21,674
For haiku, it's twenty-seven to eighty-one
percent before, and seventy to one hundred

114
00:07:21,674 --> 00:07:22,017
after.

115
00:07:22,517 --> 00:07:25,792
Those two ranges overlap, by eleven
points.

116
00:07:26,292 --> 00:07:30,480
For terra, it's forty-four to
ninety-seven, and seventy to one hundred,

117
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:31,542
which overlap too.

118
00:07:32,767 --> 00:07:37,682
The last step is to say what the result
supports, and no more, in one sentence per

119
00:07:37,682 --> 00:07:38,042
model.

120
00:07:38,542 --> 00:07:42,705
For haiku, ours says the judge's passes
went from five of nine to nine of nine,

121
00:07:42,705 --> 00:07:46,342
and that these trials can't yet tell
whether the improvement is real.

122
00:07:46,842 --> 00:07:51,010
For terra, it says they went from five of
six to nine of nine, and that these trials

123
00:07:51,010 --> 00:07:53,392
can't tell whether the rule made any
difference.

124
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:57,525
So what should we make of those two
sentences?

125
00:07:58,025 --> 00:08:02,475
On haiku, four trials failed before the
edit, three of them on one request.

126
00:08:02,975 --> 00:08:05,275
After the edit, none failed.

127
00:08:05,775 --> 00:08:08,700
Even so, the two ranges still overlap.

128
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:13,722
Nine trials a side are too few to pull
them apart, and more trials are the way to

129
00:08:13,722 --> 00:08:14,225
find out.

130
00:08:14,725 --> 00:08:18,934
On terra, only one finished trial failed
before the edit, so there was almost

131
00:08:18,934 --> 00:08:22,050
nothing left to fix, and nothing these
trials could show.

132
00:08:22,550 --> 00:08:26,762
Neither result is a failure of the edit:
both are honest statements of what a

133
00:08:26,762 --> 00:08:28,950
handful of trials can and can't tell us.

134
00:08:30,167 --> 00:08:33,692
Now let's apply the same honesty to the
earlier fixes.

135
00:08:34,192 --> 00:08:38,297
When we added the rule for follow-up
requests, haiku's conversation test went

136
00:08:38,297 --> 00:08:40,217
from two of three to three of three.

137
00:08:40,717 --> 00:08:45,070
When we rewrote the skill's description,
the loading test had no failure left to

138
00:08:45,070 --> 00:08:49,315
fix: terra passed all eleven trials that
finished before, and eleven of twelve

139
00:08:49,315 --> 00:08:49,642
after.

140
00:08:50,142 --> 00:08:53,742
In the calculator, the ranges overlap for
both fixes.

141
00:08:54,242 --> 00:08:57,317
So those runs didn't prove that the fixes
worked.

142
00:08:57,817 --> 00:09:01,767
What made the follow-up rule reasonable
was something else: a patch showed what

143
00:09:01,767 --> 00:09:05,717
caused the failure, the rule addressed
exactly that cause, and the result moved

144
00:09:05,717 --> 00:09:06,417
the right way.

145
00:09:06,917 --> 00:09:11,048
That's a sound reason to keep a rule, but
it isn't the same as having measured it,

146
00:09:11,048 --> 00:09:13,467
and it's worth knowing which of the two we
have.

147
00:09:14,667 --> 00:09:18,832
Even our clearest early fix needs this
care: on terra, the grader for made-up

148
00:09:18,832 --> 00:09:23,213
numbers went from one of three to three of
three, and alone, those ranges overlap

149
00:09:23,213 --> 00:09:23,592
widely.

150
00:09:24,092 --> 00:09:28,023
What makes that result strong is
everything around it: five of five trials

151
00:09:28,023 --> 00:09:32,273
with the same failure before the fix, and
the same stimulus passing in every run

152
00:09:32,273 --> 00:09:32,592
since.

153
00:09:33,092 --> 00:09:36,892
The run with no skill at all showed that
the first draft caused it.

154
00:09:37,392 --> 00:09:41,770
Several small results that agree, a cause
we can see in a patch, and a comparison

155
00:09:41,770 --> 00:09:45,067
with no skill: that's what a result we can
defend looks like.

156
00:09:46,267 --> 00:09:50,942
One more caution: every count here is only
as good as the grader that produced it.

157
00:09:51,442 --> 00:09:55,528
If a grader passes work that's wrong, a
range around its count is a careful

158
00:09:55,528 --> 00:09:57,217
measurement of the wrong thing.

159
00:09:57,717 --> 00:10:01,936
So we test the graders first, with the
oracle and with saved trials, and only

160
00:10:01,936 --> 00:10:03,142
then work out a range.

161
00:10:04,367 --> 00:10:07,867
One row of the experiment's table hasn't
come up yet.

162
00:10:08,367 --> 00:10:12,323
Beneath the counts are each variant's
tokens, added up over all of its trials;

163
00:10:12,323 --> 00:10:15,367
they're the agent's tokens, and the
judge's aren't included.

164
00:10:15,867 --> 00:10:20,006
In our run, the two models differ from
each other by more than the edit changed

165
00:10:20,006 --> 00:10:20,792
either of them.

166
00:10:21,292 --> 00:10:25,817
What that difference costs, for each trial
that passes, is a question for later.

167
00:10:27,033 --> 00:10:30,283
Before you go on: a count goes up after an
edit.

168
00:10:30,783 --> 00:10:33,083
What do we do before believing it?

169
00:10:36,083 --> 00:10:40,437
We compare like with like: the same
stimuli, graders and model, before and

170
00:10:40,437 --> 00:10:41,908
after, in one experiment.

171
00:10:42,408 --> 00:10:45,708
We count trials, not stimuli or
percentages.

172
00:10:46,208 --> 00:10:49,008
We work out the range around each count.

173
00:10:49,508 --> 00:10:53,683
Finally, if the ranges overlap, we say
that these trials can't tell.

174
00:10:54,900 --> 00:10:59,222
You can now compare a skill before and
after an edit, and say in one sentence

175
00:10:59,222 --> 00:11:00,625
what the result supports.

176
00:11:01,125 --> 00:11:05,800
The same table also printed tokens for
each variant, and they differ by model.

177
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:08,825
So what does a passing trial cost?

178
00:11:09,325 --> 00:11:11,175
That's the next lesson.

179
00:11:11,675 --> 00:11:15,825
For practice, the exercises on the lesson
page are worth doing first.
